From ad63c9b2ea2b5c4cb221189916325c5c1d12a564 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shammah Chancellor <shammah.chancellor@notvalid.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:33:08 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] primitives: keep only long-script bytes in CTransaction
 storage

A CTransaction stored its whole serialization next to its decoded inputs
and outputs, so prevouts, sequences, values, length prefixes and short
scripts were each stored twice. Only scripts too long for CScript's
inline buffer need bytes of their own.

Store those scripts' bytes after the outputs instead, and let them borrow
from there:

  [ control block + CTransaction | CTxIn[] | CTxOut[] | long scripts ]

- The hash is computed from the incoming bytes while the transaction is
  built, and the serialized size is cached for GetTotalSize() and
  SizeComputer. GetSerialization() is removed.
- Serialize() writes the fields as SerializeTransaction does, into a
  per-thread buffer of exactly the cached size, and hands the stream a
  single write. Serializing block 413567's transactions into a DataStream
  takes about 53 ns each, against 127 ns for upstream's field-by-field
  writes and 20 ns when the serialization was stored.
- CTxIn places nSequence directly after prevout, filling its tail padding
  (80 to 72 bytes on LP64). Serialization still writes prevout, scriptSig,
  nSequence, so no serialized bytes change.
- CTransaction keeps a 32-bit serialized size and a flag saying whether it
  owns its storage (which starts at vin.data()) instead of pointers:
  200 to 184 bytes.

Code that reads tx.vin and tx.vout is unchanged, and the transaction is
still one allocation, built without heap allocations. Block 413567's
mempool memory accounting drops from 1.96 MB to 1.56 MB, below the
std::vector layout's 1.75 MB.
